# Runs `pnpm browser:watch` on the deployment host, in a browser that is genuinely foregrounded.
#
# ## Why this image is not the bench image with a browser added
#
# The blocker this exists to clear is not "there was no browser". A browser was driven against this
# client on 2026-08-03 and it reported the player 578 seconds behind live, which was the harness and
# not the deployment: the page was permanently `visibilityState: hidden`, and Chromium responds to a
# hidden page by pausing muted video and throttling timers to about one a minute once playback
# stalls. hls.js loads fragments off those timers.
#
# So the requirement is a page that is really visible, and that is what the Xvfb entrypoint below
# buys. A headless browser would report `visible` too, and would be the easier thing to build, but
# then the harness's own visibility check would be attesting to a default rather than to the
# arrangement that makes the run valid.
#
# ## ⛔ Why Google Chrome and not Playwright's Chromium, AND WHY THAT REASON HAS EXPIRED
#
# This installed Google Chrome because Playwright's bundled Chromium was the open-source build,
# without H.264 or AAC. It would load the page, run hls.js, fetch every segment out of Swarm and
# decode none of them, surfacing as a blank picture and a player that never advances, which is what a
# delivery failure looks like: a day's investigation pointed at the wrong layer.
#
# Playwright v1.57 replaced the bundled Chromium with Chrome for Testing, which has shipped the
# proprietary codecs since 119. Measured 2026-08-11 on linux64, which is what this file builds, with
# the pinned playwright-core 1.61.1 and its own browser revision 1228 / 149.0.7827.55:
# `MediaSource.isTypeSupported` and `canPlayType` both answer for `avc1.42E01E` and `mp4a.40.2`,
# while a deliberately bogus codec answers false, so the probe discriminates rather than agreeing
# with everything. Verified on x86-64 only; the v1.57 notes keep Arm64 Linux on Chromium.
#
# So the reason below no longer chooses anything, and nothing is invented to replace it. The open
# decision is whether to drop this apt repository for Chrome for Testing, whose archive keeps known
# good builds rather than current stable only, which would make the browser version pinned by
# playwright-core in the lockfile instead of unpinnable. That would also remove the signing key and
# roughly half this image.
#
# `e2e/src/browser/instrument.ts` asserts the codecs at runtime rather than trusting this comment,
# which is the property that made it safe to re-examine.
FROM node:22-bookworm-slim

# Chrome from Google's own repository, verified against their signing key. `xvfb` is the display the
# browser is foregrounded on, and `fonts-liberation` is what stops the clock overlay and the client's
# own text rendering as boxes in the screenshots the run is read from.
#
# `xauth` is listed because `--no-install-recommends` does not pull it and `xvfb-run` shells out to
# it for every invocation. Without it the entrypoint fails before Chrome is reached, which reads as
# the harness being broken rather than as a missing package.
RUN apt-get update \
  && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends wget gnupg ca-certificates \
  && wget -qO /usr/share/keyrings/google-chrome.asc https://dl-ssl.google.com/linux/linux_signing_key.pub \
  && echo "deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/google-chrome.asc] https://dl-ssl.google.com/linux/chrome/deb/ stable main" \
     > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/google-chrome.list \
  && apt-get update \
  && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends google-chrome-stable xvfb xauth fonts-liberation \
  && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
  && google-chrome-stable --version

# Deliberately not pinned. Google's apt repository keeps only current stable, so a pin here would
# break the image on their release cadence rather than on ours. The version that actually ran is read
# off the browser at runtime and printed in every report, which is the property that matters: a
# reading can always be attributed to a build, even though the build is not chosen here.

# `curl` is what `Host.localJson` shells out to, and the docker client is how a crash scenario stops
# and starts the container it is injecting a fault into. Both talk to the host over what
# `bench-on-host.sh` already mounts, so this adds a client and no daemon. Pinned, and taken from the
# static tarball for the reason `Dockerfile.bench` gives: the alternative is adding docker's apt
# repository to pull one binary.
#
# A crash scenario has to watch the viewer and move the infrastructure in the same program, because
# what it measures is the interval between the two. Split across a shell script and a report reader,
# the fault's time and the sample's time come from different clocks on different machines, and the
# quantity being measured is a few seconds.
# `iproute2` is `tc` and `ip`, which `deploy/scripts/shape-container-ingress.sh` needs to put a real
# ingress policer on this container's own interface. ⛔ It belongs in THIS image and not only in
# `Dockerfile.bench`: `browser-on-host.sh` builds this one, so a shaped viewer arm runs here, and
# the shaper refuses the run rather than measuring an unshaped link when `tc` is absent.
ARG DOCKER_CLI_VERSION=27.3.1
RUN apt-get update \
  && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl iproute2 \
  && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
  && curl -fsSL "https://download.docker.com/linux/static/stable/x86_64/docker-${DOCKER_CLI_VERSION}.tgz" \
  | tar -xz -C /usr/local/bin --strip-components=1 docker/docker \
  && docker --version

# Pinned to what the repository is developed against, for the reasons `Dockerfile.bench` records at
# length: pnpm 11 reads this workspace's overrides as absent and refuses a frozen install, and a
# corepack activation done as root at build time is invisible to the invoking user at run time.
ARG PNPM_VERSION=9.12.0
RUN npm install -g "pnpm@${PNPM_VERSION}" && pnpm --version

# The X server puts its socket here and cannot create the directory itself, because the container
# runs as the invoking user rather than as root. Created at build time, when there is still a root to
# create it with.
RUN mkdir -p /tmp/.X11-unix && chmod 1777 /tmp/.X11-unix

WORKDIR /repo

# Every command runs on a real X display. As an entrypoint rather than something each call has to
# remember, so that forgetting it is not a way to get a plausible-looking reading out of a browser
# that was never foregrounded.
COPY browser-entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/browser-entrypoint.sh
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/browser-entrypoint.sh"]
